                         Patch Metadata - Gen 6/80 - Novelty: 1/2 - Resample: 1/3 - Patch: 1/2                          
╭──────────────────────────┬───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
│ Field                    │ Value                                                                                     │
├──────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ patch_type               │ full                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ none                                                                                      │
│ patch_description        │ Instead of giving every qubit its own independent rotation angle (the previous seed used  │
│                          │ 16–24 unique parameters per block), this design exploits deliberate parameter sharing     │
│                          │ across qubit groups to drastically cut the trainable parameter count while preserving     │
│                          │ expressive power through entanglement.                                                    │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ Key ideas:                                                                                │
│                          │ 1. **Grouped rotation sharing** – The 8 qubits are split into 4 groups of 2 (wire % 4),   │
│                          │ and each group shares a single RY parameter and a single RZ parameter. This cuts the      │
│                          │ single-qubit rotation parameter count from 16 to 8 per block while still letting          │
│                          │ different subsets of qubits behave differently (since encoding already gives each qubit   │
│                          │ distinct input data; the trainable layer only needs to provide a coarser correction).     │
│                          │ 2. **Fixed CZ ring entangler** – A full ring of CZ gates (0-1,1-2,...,7-0) mixes          │
│                          │ information between all qubits with zero extra trainable parameters, providing the same   │
│                          │ entangling power as a linear chain but with full connectivity (i.e., wrap-around          │
│                          │ coupling).                                                                                │
│                          │ 3. **Shared controlled-rotation ring** – A ring of CRZ gates, all sharing ONE trainable   │
│                          │ parameter, adds a global, tunable non-linear coupling strength between neighboring        │
│                          │ qubits. This lets the network learn a single "entanglement strength" knob that            │
│                          │ participates in all pairwise correlations, adding expressivity at the cost of just one    │
│                          │ more parameter.                                                                           │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ Total unique trainable parameter names per block: 4 (RY groups) + 4 (RZ groups) + 1       │
│                          │ (shared CRZ) = 9, versus 16 in the current best program (98 total params) and 24 in the   │
│                          │ original seed (146 total params). This should preserve near-perfect accuracy (since       │
│                          │ problem is apparently easily separable given the fixed encoding) while substantially      │
│                          │ improving parameter economy, and likely converge in fewer optimization steps due to the   │
│                          │ smaller, better-conditioned parameter space.                                              │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ This is architecturally different from the seed's "independent-per-wire rotation" scheme: │
│                          │ it fuses aggressive parameter tying with a topologically richer (ring, not chain)         │
│                          │ entangling structure and adds a single global trainable coupling term instead of per-wire │
│                          │ trainable phases, testing whether shared coarse-grained control suffices for the          │
│                          │ underlying classification task.                                                           │
