│ patch_type               │ full                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ none                                                                                      │
│ patch_description        │ Building on the previous group-shared RY/RZ ansatz (4 shared parameters, score 0.66),     │
│                          │ this version pushes parameter economy to its logical extreme following the explicit       │
│                          │ recommendation: collapse all 8 pre-entanglement RY rotations into a single shared         │
│                          │ parameter `ry_global`, and all 8 post-entanglement RZ rotations into a single shared      │
│                          │ parameter `rz_global`. This cuts the unique-parameter count per block from 4 to 2 (from   │
│                          │ n_params=26 down to n_params=14 given the fixed 6x repetition + 2 readout params observed │
│                          │ empirically), testing the lower bound of expressivity for the RY-CZ-RZ template.          │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ To compensate for the loss of per-qubit rotation flexibility, the entangling layer is     │
│                          │ upgraded from an open nearest-neighbor CZ chain to a closed ring (adding the wrap-around  │
│                          │ CZ between qubit 7 and qubit 0). This costs zero extra parameters but increases the       │
│                          │ connectivity/expressivity of the entangling layer, helping preserve the ability to        │
│                          │ correlate all qubits despite the coarser rotation sharing. The insight driving this       │
│                          │ design: with only 8 qubits, a ring topology gives every qubit exactly two entangling      │
│                          │ neighbors (versus one for the chain endpoints), evening out the entangling power across   │
│                          │ all wires while keeping gate count and depth roughly the same as before.                  │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ This directly tests whether near-perfect accuracy and zero generalization gap can be      │
│                          │ sustained with an extreme reduction in trainable parameters, maximizing the               │
│                          │ parameter-economy term of the score while relying on the fixed repetition structure       │
│                          │ (independent copies per block) to still supply enough total capacity across repetitions.  │
