                         Patch Metadata - Gen 23/80 - Novelty: 1/2 - Resample: 1/3 - Patch: 1/2                         
╭──────────────────────────┬───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
│ Field                    │ Value                                                                                     │
├──────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ patch_type               │ full                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ none                                                                                      │
│ patch_description        │ Prior generations showed that collapsing the trainable-parameter set to just 2 unique     │
│                          │ values (either two global RY/RZ angles, or two group angles reused across RY/RZ) already  │
│                          │ reaches perfect train/val/test accuracy (score 0.72, n_params=14). The 4-unique-parameter │
│                          │ variant only hurt score (0.66) despite identical accuracy, confirming the score is        │
│                          │ dominated by parameter economy once accuracy saturates. This version pushes economy       │
│                          │ further: a single scalar angle "theta" drives every RY gate in the pre-entanglement layer │
│                          │ and every RZ gate in the post-entanglement layer (reused, not separate), combined with a  │
│                          │ lightweight open nearest-neighbor CZ chain (no wrap-around) for entanglement, which       │
│                          │ previously gave the fastest convergence (30 steps) and lowest depth (29) among            │
│                          │ equally-accurate designs. This yields only 1 trainable parameter per block repetition     │
│                          │ (n_params expected ≈ 8, versus 14 for the previous best), maximizing parameter_efficiency │
