╭──────────────────────────┬───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
│ Field                    │ Value                                                                                     │
├──────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ patch_type               │ cross                                                                                     │
│ patch_name               │ none                                                                                      │
│ patch_description        │ This design merges the strongest traits of the two lineages:                              │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ 1. From the "seed 2" line (score 0.72, depth 29): coarse 2-group parameter sharing        │
│                          │ (`p_g0` for qubits 0-3, `p_g1` for qubits 4-7) applied both pre- and post-entanglement,   │
│                          │ and a shallow linear nearest-neighbor entangling chain (no extra wrap-around/2-skip       │
│                          │ links), which achieved perfect accuracy with only 2 unique parameters per block and the   │
│                          │ lowest circuit depth among the 0.72-scoring variants.                                     │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ 2. From the "current" program's idea of parametrized two-qubit interaction (using         │
│                          │ `XX`/`CRZ`-type gates instead of fixed `CZ`) to inject tunable entanglement strength —    │
│                          │ but crucially, instead of introducing a *new* parameter name (which raised the            │
│                          │ unique-parameter count to 3 and empirically hurt both parameter-efficiency and            │
│                          │ convergence speed, dropping the score to 0.68), the new entangling layer *reuses* the     │
│                          │ already-existing `p_g0`/`p_g1` names, alternating between them along the chain.           │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ This gives the circuit a trainable, more expressive entangling layer (as opposed to a     │
│                          │ completely fixed `CZ` chain) while keeping the total trainable-parameter count at exactly │
│                          │ 2 per block — matching the best-known parameter economy. The linear (non-wraparound)      │
│                          │ topology keeps depth/gate-count low, consistent with the better-depth 0.72 variant rather │
│                          │ than the deeper 2-skip-ring variant (which scored identically but at higher depth/gate    │
│                          │ cost, offering no benefit).                                                               │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ The hypothesis is that reusing existing parameters for the entangling gates increases     │
│                          │ expressivity/accuracy-robustness on very few training examples without paying any         │
│                          │ parameter-economy or convergence-speed penalty, potentially improving on the 0.72         │
│                          │ baseline.                                                                                 │
