│ patch_type               │ full                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ none                                                                                      │
│ patch_description        │ Builds on the highest-scoring prior design (single globally-shared "theta" parameter      │
│                          │ driving both RY and RZ layers, combined with a fixed CZ ring + diameter connections)      │
│                          │ which achieved the best economy/accuracy trade-off (score 0.80, n_params=8). The key      │
│                          │ insight from comparing generations is that *parameter count dominates the score*, while   │
│                          │ additional fixed (parameter-free) entangling connectivity or extra rotation axes reusing  │
│                          │ the *same* parameter name can add expressive power and speed convergence without any      │
│                          │ extra trainable-parameter cost.                                                           │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ This version pushes that insight further: it keeps exactly one trainable parameter name   │
│                          │ ("theta") per block copy (matching the best n_params=8 setup), but now uses **three**     │
│                          │ rotation axes (RY, RZ, RX) all sharing "theta", interleaved with **three** different      │
│                          │ fixed entangling topologies (nearest-neighbor ring, antipodal "diameter" pairs, and       │
│                          │ skip-2 twisted ring) for much richer, near-all-to-all connectivity at zero extra          │
│                          │ parameter cost. The intuition: more diverse fixed entanglement combined with more         │
│                          │ rotation axes reusing a single scalar parameter increases the Hilbert-space trajectory    │
│                          │ reachable by a 1-parameter family per repetition, which should improve convergence speed  │
│                          │ and robustness while preserving the maximal parameter economy that drove the 0.80 score.  │
