╭──────────────────────────┬───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
│ Field                    │ Value                                                                                     │
├──────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ patch_type               │ cross                                                                                     │
│ patch_name               │ ring_antipodal_crz_single_param                                                           │
│ patch_description        │ Crossover of the two top-performing seeds (both achieving score 0.80 with a single shared │
│                          │ parameter "theta") and the CRZ-augmented seed (score 0.72, but showed that adding a       │
│                          │ parametrized entangling layer can slightly help expressivity when it doesn't add extra    │
│                          │ unique parameters).                                                                       │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ Design rationale:                                                                         │
│                          │ 1. Keep the minimal-parameter philosophy that drove both 0.80-scoring seeds: every        │
│                          │ single-qubit rotation (RX, RY, RZ) reuses the SAME single trainable parameter "theta".    │
│                          │ This keeps `n_params` equal to the number of block repetitions (best parameter economy    │
│                          │ observed).                                                                                │
│                          │ 2. Use the denser, cheaper fixed entangling topology from the fastest-converging seed     │
│                          │ (ring of nearest-neighbor CZ + antipodal CZ pairs), which gave `convergence_step_mean:    │
│                          │ 60` and the lowest losses, instead of the ring+2-skip-twisted-ring combo (which converged │
│                          │ more slowly at 90 steps) or the disjoint-pair CRZ with an extra "phi" parameter (which    │
│                          │ hurt parameter efficiency, dropping the score to 0.72).                                   │
│                          │ 3. Borrow the *idea* of adding a parametrized entangling gate (CRZ) from the CRZ-seed to  │
│                          │ inject a richer, non-commuting entangling interaction on top of the fixed CZ              │
│                          │ connectivity, but critically reuse the existing "theta" parameter (never introduce a new  │
│                          │ "phi"), so this richer structure comes at ZERO extra parameter cost. This mirrors the     │
│                          │ first seed's construction, which also reused theta for its CRZ layer, but pairs the CRZ   │
│                          │ gates with the antipodal partners already used for the fixed CZ layer, keeping depth      │
│                          │ minimal and directly reinforcing the same entangling structure with a tunable strength.   │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ This combination is designed to retain the best parameter economy (single shared theta,   │
│                          │ n_params = number of block reps) and the fast convergence / low loss behavior of the      │
│                          │ antipodal-CZ seed, while adding modest extra expressivity from the reused-parameter CRZ   │
│                          │ layer, in the hope of matching or exceeding the 0.80 score with equal or better           │
│                          │ generalization and convergence speed.                                                     │
│ num_applied              │ 1                                                                                         │
