                         Patch Metadata - Gen 48/80 - Novelty: 1/2 - Resample: 1/3 - Patch: 1/2                         
╭──────────────────────────┬───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
│ Field                    │ Value                                                                                     │
├──────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ patch_type               │ cross                                                                                     │
│ patch_name               │ none                                                                                      │
│ patch_description        │ Cross-over of the two 0.80-scoring seeds (single shared-parameter RX-RY-RZ rotation stack │
│                          │ with dense CZ ring/skip/antipodal entanglers) with the alternating-CZ/XX idea from the    │
│                          │ third program. The key insight from the metrics is that using a *single* globally-shared  │
│                          │ trainable parameter ("theta") for all rotation axes keeps parameter_efficiency high       │
│                          │ (n_params=8 total across block repetitions gave score 0.80, vs n_params=20 giving only    │
│                          │ 0.68), while a **fixed** (parameter-free) long-range entangling topology (ring + partial  │
│                          │ skip-2 sub-ring + antipodal shortcuts) rather than a *newly parametrized* CRZ antipodal   │
│                          │ layer gives noticeably faster convergence (60 steps vs 120 steps) at identical accuracy.  │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ To push this further we:                                                                  │
│                          │ 1. Keep exactly ONE trainable parameter name ("theta") reused across RX, RY and RZ layers │
│                          │ (maximal parameter economy, matching the best-scoring seeds).                             │
│                          │ 2. Keep the fast-converging entangling topology from the second seed: full                │
│                          │ nearest-neighbor CZ ring (8 links, zero param), a lighter skip-2 sub-ring on every other  │
│                          │ qubit (4 links, zero param) and antipodal shortcuts (4 links, zero param) — this graph    │
│                          │ already gave 1.00 accuracy with fast (60-step) convergence and low depth.                 │
│                          │ 3. Inject one *additional* non-commuting two-qubit interaction by upgrading half of the   │
│                          │ nearest-neighbor ring links from CZ to a parametrized "XX" gate that REUSES the same      │
│                          │ shared "theta" parameter (an idea borrowed from the "current" program's alternating-CZ/XX │
│                          │ ring, but critically without introducing a new parameter name, so total unique trainable  │
│                          │ parameters remains exactly 1). This adds expressive power / tunable entanglement strength │
│                          │ while keeping the parameter-economy advantage that drove the 0.80 score.                  │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ This combination keeps n_params minimal (1 shared name per block => same param-efficiency │
│                          │ class as the best seeds), reuses the fast, low-depth entangling graph that empirically    │
│                          │ converged in fewer steps, and adds a cheap (no extra parameter) boost to expressivity via │
│                          │ shared-parameter XX gates on alternating ring edges.                                      │
