│ patch_description        │ Both prior 0.80-scoring seeds used a single block-shared parameter name                   │
│                          │ ("p_shared"/"theta") reused across every rotation and entangling gate, keeping n_params   │
│                          │ at the architecture minimum (one independent copy per block repetition). The current      │
│                          │ 20-parameter variant (3 unique names: p_g0, p_g1, xx_ent) dropped the score to 0.68       │
│                          │ purely from parameter-economy loss, even though accuracy stayed perfect — confirming that │
│                          │ unique-parameter count, not accuracy, is the main lever available to improve the score    │
│                          │ further given accuracy is already saturated at 1.0.                                       │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ This version keeps the proven single-shared-parameter design (n_params = minimum          │
│                          │ architecture floor, same as both 0.80 seeds) but tightens the circuit: it uses three      │
│                          │ rotation axes (RX, RY, RZ) all bound to one shared name "theta" for maximal non-commuting │
│                          │ single-qubit expressivity per parameter, combined with a leaner entangling backbone — a   │
│                          │ nearest-neighbor CZ ring plus a sparse 4-edge "antipodal" CZ set (instead of a denser     │
│                          │ dual-ring + antipodal combo) — and a single shared-parameter CRZ layer at offset 2 for a  │
│                          │ parametrized non-local interaction. This reduces total gate count and depth relative to   │
│                          │ both prior 0.80 seeds while preserving the same non-commuting 3-axis-rotation +           │
│                          │ fixed-entangle + parametrized-entangle structure that gave perfect train/val/test         │
│                          │ accuracy, aiming to improve convergence speed and/or loss without paying any              │
│                          │ parameter-count penalty.                                                                  │
