                         Patch Metadata - Gen 53/80 - Novelty: 1/2 - Resample: 1/3 - Patch: 1/2                         
╭──────────────────────────┬───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
│ Field                    │ Value                                                                                     │
├──────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ patch_type               │ cross                                                                                     │
│ patch_name               │ none                                                                                      │
│ patch_description        │ Crossover between the "single shared-parameter" seed (low gate-count, low loss, but slow  │
│                          │ convergence) and the "dual-axis RX/RY + antipodal CRZ" seed (fast convergence, but        │
│                          │ heavier gate count). Both prior variants use only ONE trainable parameter name            │
│                          │ ("theta"/"p_shared") reused across every rotation and even across the parametrized        │
│                          │ entangler, so the total parameter count is driven entirely by the fixed number of block   │
│                          │ repetitions applied outside this file (all three reference programs reach n_params = 8).  │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ The key design choice here is to keep the single unique parameter name but assemble a     │
│                          │ *lean* single-block circuit (no redundant block-doubling like the current program, which  │
│                          │ merely doubles depth/gate-count for zero benefit) that borrows:                           │
│                          │   * the RX -> RY dual-axis pre-rotation (non-commuting axes) from the fast-converging     │
│                          │ seed, which appears responsible for its low convergence_step (60 steps) since the extra   │
│                          │ axis gives the shared scalar more effective expressive directions before entanglement,    │
│                          │   * a single fixed CZ ring (cheap, zero-parameter entangling backbone) from the           │
│                          │ low-gate-count / low-loss seed instead of the heavier ring+skip-2+alternating-CNOT        │
│                          │ combination,                                                                              │
│                          │   * a small antipodal CRZ layer reusing "theta" (only 4 extra gates) to inject one        │
│                          │ tunable long-range entangling interaction, since this was present in the fast-converging  │
│                          │ seed and likely contributes to its quick convergence,                                     │
│                          │   * the RZ post-rotation reusing "theta" to close out the block, as in all three seeds.   │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ This drops the gate count drastically (from 168–432 down to ~36 gates per block) and      │
│                          │ depth accordingly, while retaining the multi-axis rotation diversity and one tunable      │
│                          │ entangling gate that seemed to drive fast convergence, aiming for a design that keeps     │
│                          │ perfect train/val/test accuracy and low loss but converges faster and is far cheaper in   │
│                          │ depth/gate-count than any of the three prior variants — improving parameter economy and   │
│                          │ circuit efficiency without adding any new trainable parameter names.                      │
