                         Patch Metadata - Gen 54/80 - Novelty: 1/2 - Resample: 1/3 - Patch: 1/2                         
╭──────────────────────────┬───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
│ Field                    │ Value                                                                                     │
├──────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ simplify_shared_theta_block                                                               │
│ patch_description        │ Analysis of the three prior variants shows the score plateaus at 0.80 whenever n_params=8 │
│                          │ (1 shared trainable parameter "theta" per repetition) and accuracy is already perfect     │
│                          │ (1.0) across train/val/test with zero generalization gap. This implies                    │
│                          │ parameter_efficiency ≈ accuracy/n_params is the dominant score driver, and since we're    │
│                          │ already at the minimal nonzero parameter floor (1 unique name × fixed repetition count),  │
│                          │ adding a second parameter (as suggested by the naive "two thetas" recommendation) would   │
│                          │ double n_params and likely hurt parameter efficiency without any accuracy gain (accuracy  │
│                          │ is already saturated at 1.0).                                                             │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ Instead, I simplify the block to reduce depth and gate count while keeping exactly one    │
│                          │ shared parameter name "theta", mirroring the leanest-performing seed (which had the       │
│                          │ lowest validation/test loss, 0.01, at depth 84 and gate count 168) but retaining the      │
│                          │ antipodal long-range connectivity that seemed to help convergence speed (60 steps) in the │
│                          │ current version. Concretely, I drop the redundant "skip-2" fixed CZ layer (2b) since it   │
│                          │ adds gate count/depth without touching parameter count or without clear benefit over the  │
│                          │ plain ring + antipodal connections, keeping the circuit shallower and hopefully           │
│                          │ converging faster and generalizing at least as well, which may nudge the secondary score  │
│                          │ terms (loss, convergence, depth) upward without risking the already-saturated accuracy or │
│                          │ the parameter-economy floor.                                                              │
│ num_applied              │ 1                                                                                         │
│ api_costs                │ $0.0770                                                                                   │
│ error_attempt            │ None                                                                                      │
│ system_prompt_id         │ None                                                                                      │
│ model_name               │ openrouter/anthropic/claude-sonnet-5                                                      │
│ temperature              │ 1.0                                                                                       │
│ max_output_tokens        │ 16384                                                                                     │
│ diff_summary             │ added: 0; deleted: 7; modified: 0;                                                        │
