                         Patch Metadata - Gen 57/80 - Novelty: 1/2 - Resample: 1/3 - Patch: 1/2                         
╭──────────────────────────┬───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
│ Field                    │ Value                                                                                     │
├──────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ patch_type               │ full                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ dual_shared_theta_compact                                                                 │
│ patch_description        │ Building on the observation that all prior single-shared-parameter ("theta") designs      │
│                          │ plateau at a 0.80 combined score with perfect train/val/test accuracy, this version tests │
│                          │ the recommended two-shared-parameter split: one scalar "theta_r" drives all single-qubit  │
│                          │ rotations (RX, RY, RZ axes reused across every qubit) while a second, independent scalar  │
│                          │ "theta_e" drives a single non-local parametrized entangling sweep (CRZ at distance-2).    │
│                          │ This gives the optimizer a genuinely separate rotation-vs-entanglement degree of freedom  │
│                          │ (only 2 unique parameter names per block instead of 1) while simultaneously *shrinking*   │
│                          │ the circuit: the heavy multi-layer fixed entangling stack (nearest-neighbor CZ ring +     │
│                          │ skip-2 CZ ring + antipodal CZ shortcuts + parametrized CRZ sweep) used in earlier         │
│                          │ variants is collapsed into a single nearest-neighbor CZ ring (for baseline connectivity,  │
│                          │ zero-param) plus one parametrized CRZ skip-2 sweep (for tunable long-range entanglement). │
│                          │ This roughly halves gate count and depth relative to the current program (from ~264 gates │
│                          │ / depth 114 down to ~40 gates / depth ~40), which should speed up convergence and reduce  │
│                          │ any residual optimization difficulty, while keeping parameter count as low as physically  │
│                          │ sensible (2 unique names per block) and preserving the full 3-axis single-qubit rotation  │
│                          │ basis and ring+long-range entangling topology that empirically achieved perfect accuracy  │
│                          │ in prior seeds.                                                                           │
│ num_applied              │ 1                                                                                         │
│ api_costs                │ $0.0486                                                                                   │
